From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:40:13 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 39/46] hjviewer: minimum version: Windows PCs install the
 newer version

An installed Windows PC is updated by its own service, with nobody at it,
and an HJViewer that was only started fetches the new file and hands over to
it (minimum version design 5-1 to 5-3, 6-5). What and when it installs
follow the trust rules, as the app's own verdict does. Four one-line hooks
in shared files (M8, M9, M11, M12) and small ones in upstream's packer (P1,
P2 and its wait), the rest in src/hjviewer_update and
libs/portable/src/hjv_guard.rs:

- install_plan.rs: the rules, which touch nothing and are tested on any
  platform. When a PC installs: in a rollout at auto_install_after plus its
  ring's days plus its spread (of H = SHA-256("hjv-ring-v1" NUL device ID) the
  ring is H[0] mod 3 and the spread the 24-bit number H[1..4] scaled to a
  day); below a minimum, a PC in its grace period (at or above grace_min) at
  the deadline plus the spread, and a PC below grace_min, which is blocked
  already, at the policy's issued plus the spread; never a version at or
  below its own, never without a package. Sessions hold an install up,
  except one a person asked for, and one that is a day overdue below the
  minimum, which goes ahead in the quiet hours (02:00 to 05:00 where the PC
  is) after ten minutes of warning. The record of the tries: the installer of
  one package is started three times at most, six hours apart; a download
  that fails is no install and is tried again after half an hour, two hours,
  then every six; a package that is not the signed one, or whose own version
  is not the one the policy names for it, is left for a day, a PC the server
  hands nothing to asks again after six hours, or as soon as a login binds
  it; another package or a person's request starts the count anew, and a
  clock set back holds nothing up for longer than these waits. A PC is bound
  when the account module's state of it says so: empty, "revoked" and
  "needs-relogin" are no binding. The file's own version
  (file_version_holds): the first three numbers of its fixed version
  (VS_FIXEDFILEINFO) must be the target version; the Windows package is the
  packer, which scripts/set-client-version.sh builds with the app's version
  and winres writes with 0 as its fourth number (package.build is the
  installed app's file version and is not compared), so that a signed
  package under another version's label is not run. The names: the file is
  HJViewer-<version>.exe in a folder of eight random hex digits, the command
  is "<path>" --update with the path quoted, a plain full path of a drive
  that the packer does not read as "install" or "quick support", and below
  260 UTF-16 units (platform/windows.cc copies it into MAX_PATH). A signal
  counts once, at most every ten seconds, and every ten minutes after one
  that fetched a whole package. What the main window reads of the service's
  option comes out as known words only; a window that asked waits for the
  service to name its signal, twenty seconds at most, not counting an update
  the service is in the middle of, and from then on shows what the service
  says.
- install.rs: the service's update, one tick a second, with everything
  outside the process behind the trait Machine so that the order of the
  steps is tested with a stand-in. The service is given an entry only while
  the PC may install from the policy it holds (MAY_INSTALL: of the PC's
  epoch, not below the build's floor, of a key that is not revoked), never
  from a declaration's base. By itself it installs only by a policy the
  server confirmed in the last half hour, so a policy that takes a rollout
  back stops every PC that still reaches the server. It looks at the
  sessions first and asks the server only when it could install now:
  nothing is asked while sessions hold the install up, an answer older than
  half an hour is asked for again, and only a check the server did not
  answer waits six hours. A PC that hjv-api refused (403: on no account)
  and that a login then binds, in the service's process, has that refusal
  dropped from the record and asks for the package at its next look. The
  package comes from door 1 by the device key into a new file in a new
  folder; the file is closed, opened again so that nobody can write, rename
  or delete it, and read through that handle against the signed size and
  SHA-256 and its own version; the sessions are looked at a second time.
  Right before the start, after the download and after the ten-minute
  warning, the service asks the server for the policy again (a check
  somebody waits for, within those limits) and waits for the answer,
  CHECK_WAIT (40 s) at most. It starts nothing unless, by the policy it then
  holds, MAY_INSTALL still holds, the entry's latest and package are still
  the ones it fetched and, unless a person asked, the PC's time to install
  has still come and the server confirmed that policy in the last half hour:
  a declaration, a withdrawn package or a stopped rollout (runbook 5-6) that
  the server serves by then stops the install, while a PC below the minimum
  and one a person asked for go on; the file is removed and nothing is
  counted. Where sessions hold the install up, they are looked at once more
  after that answer: a session that came in meanwhile puts the try off by
  half an hour. Then the try is written down, and only then the installer
  starts, with the handle held until it runs. A file that was there before
  is never used. A process that still runs ten minutes after it started the
  installer counts the install as failed; the next process of the service
  finds either the new version, and clears up, or the old one. Also here:
  the record's file, the sweep of what downloads left (only this module's
  folders and files, no link followed), and for an app that is not
  installed the move of a checked download into a folder and the check of
  the file, its own version included, right before it is run.
- win.rs: the Windows calls, nothing that decides. The service's folder is
  hjviewer-update under FOLDERID_LocalAppData of SYSTEM (the known-folder
  call, no environment variable; never the config folder or %TEMP%), made
  with O:SY D:P(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA) from the start, and refused
  when it, or a folder above it up to the drive, is a link or junction, or
  when it belongs to anyone but SYSTEM or the administrators; the new folder
  below it must not exist yet. Files are opened with FILE_SHARE_READ alone,
  and their version is read with GetFileVersionInfoSizeW,
  GetFileVersionInfoW and VerQueryValueW. The installer starts through
  launch_privileged_process and its early exit code is read; the warning is
  WTSSendMessageW on the session's desktop. The service's thread runs only
  in --server of an installed HJViewer as SYSTEM. In the main window of an
  installed PC hjv-update-now (and hjv-update-download) writes a new value
  into the service's option hjv-update-now, and the state's job and
  credential are the service's (option hjv-update-job; credential device or
  unbound). In an app that is not installed hjv-update-download fetches by
  the login into a new folder of the user's temporary files, checks, and
  puts HJViewer-<version>.exe into the user's Downloads in place of
  whatever has that name (MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING), and
  hjv-update-run reads that file again through a handle nobody can write
  past, checks its version, and starts exactly it, with HJV_WAIT_PID set so
  that the new packer waits for this process to end. A file of another
  version is the failure code "version".
- mod.rs: the three modules, the keys hjv-update-now and hjv-update-run,
  `installed` now true only for the installed HJViewer itself (a copy
  started beside it updates like any app that is not installed),
  auto_update_option for hjviewer.rs, and the service's start in
  start_service_checks, which rendezvous_mediator.rs already calls in the
  process that takes connections (M10, with the PC's refusal of old
  controllers). door.rs: start_download takes where the checked file goes
  (`Ok` leaves it in place).
- The packer (libs/portable), for the Windows download (HJViewer.exe,
  HJViewer-install.exe) that unpacks the app into a folder of the user and
  starts it from there. While an older HJViewer still runs from that
  folder, Windows keeps its files from being replaced, and the packer went
  on regardless: it started a mix of old and new files, or, under a name
  ending in install.exe, the old version's installer (design 5-3, 2-6). In
  a build pinned to an hjviewer server (HJV_SERVER at build time) it now
  stops; any other build unpacks as before. bin_reader.rs: write_to_file
  says whether the file is in place, written or there already with the same
  MD5 (P1). main.rs: setup hands each result to the guard (P2), and main
  waits for the app that hands over to it. hjv_guard.rs: a file that is not
  in place ends the packer with exit code 32 (ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION),
  before the folder is recorded as unpacked and before anything is started.
  A message box says why (the design's K1), except in an install with
  nobody there (--update, which the service's update uses, and
  --silent-install), where a box would be read by nobody and keep the
  process from ending. An app that updates itself (win.rs) starts the new
  packer and ends; the packer waits for the process named in HJV_WAIT_PID,
  a minute at most, and two seconds for the windows it left, before it
  touches the folder; the app it starts does not inherit the variable.

The hooks: updater.rs check_update returns at once in a pinned build (M8),
common.rs do_check_software_update fails at once (M9), so no pinned build
asks api.rustdesk.com; hjviewer.rs fixes allow-auto-update to "Y" where the
build has usable update keys, which shows as a ticked, locked box (M11);
platform/windows.rs prepare_custom_client_update returns at once, so
--update reads no custom.txt that any user could have put under ProgramData
(M12). --silent-install is not used anywhere.

A build without update keys starts no thread, writes no option and has none
of the keys. M8, M9 and M12 act in every build pinned to a server, with or
without keys: such a build already asked upstream's server nothing, and now
it cannot.

Tests (cargo test --lib hjviewer_update, no network, no Windows): the slots
of fixed IDs made with Python's hashlib; the table of times and of what
sessions hold up, the anchors of a PC in its grace period and of one below
grace_min, also during a raise; the record's rules and the account states;
the file's version (the packer's, a fourth number, an older or newer build,
none, the largest version); names and commands, the 259th and 260th unit,
Korean paths, quotes, each character the command refuses on its own, and
other tricks in a path; signals; the service against a stand-in PC whose
server's last answer ages with the clock and whose checks bring a changed
policy on cue: the order of the steps, the device's request equal to the
design's research vector, sessions before and after the download, an
install soon after a long session, an answer that could not be used asked
for again, an overdue install in the next quiet hours, seven kinds of wrong
answers that are not run, a file of another version left for a day and the
right one taken a day later, bytes changed on the disk before the last
opening and while the warning is up, failed downloads, three installs and
their count kept through the waits for sessions and for the server, an
installer that does not start, a record that cannot be written, a clock set
back, a person's signal with its limits, the overdue install with and
without sessions, the policy that was not just confirmed, the policy asked
for again right before the start and an answer that does not come waited
for forty seconds, a policy that the server serves by the time the package
is in or once the warning is up (nothing to install from, the package
withdrawn, another package, a newer latest) starting nothing, a stopped
rollout stopping the rollout's install but not a forced one or one a person
asked for, a policy that grew older than half an hour during the warning
starting nothing (1800 s still installs, 1801 s does not) until the server
confirms it, a session that came in while the server was asked putting the
try off (in a rollout and a day overdue), facts() without an entry after a
declaration whose policy did not get through and under a build whose floor
is above the held policy, the next process, the PC on no account and the
PC bound after a refusal. The packer's (cargo test -p
rustdesk-portable-packer, any platform): the results of write_to_file
(written, there already, replaced, a folder in the file's place, a folder
that cannot be made), when the packer stops and when it tells, the process
ID's form, the text. Small changes to the rules and to the order of the
steps (mutations, about a hundred, one at a time) each fail a test.

Windows only, and so not compiled or run here: win.rs and the packer's
MessageBoxW, OpenProcess and WaitForSingleObject. They are type-checked for
x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, with Rust 1.75 too, in a stand-in crate that takes
the module's real files and the client's crate versions and features; what
the calls do on a PC, and what GetFileVersionInfoW reads of the real
packer, is a device test.

Known limits: a PC whose download hjv-api answered 401 (its device key is no
longer taken) waits those six hours even after a login on the PC binds it
again; only the 403 of a PC on no account is dropped at a new binding. A
person's request goes at once. When the server does not answer that last
question within 40 seconds, or the limits of checks somebody waits for
hold it back, the service decides by the policy it holds: unless a person
asked, only one the server confirmed in the last half hour.

(Minimum version design, work package C3; trust rules, work package TR.)

